course: dictionary
lesson: 939
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Bloat» is one of them. The meaning you pick depends on the context, which means the words around it. Read the whole sentence, then ask which meaning fits.
explain: As a verb (a doing word), bloat means to swell up and become bigger than normal, usually with air, gas or liquid. «Eating too fast can bloat your tummy.» Your tummy grows bigger and feels tight and full.
explain: As a noun (a naming word), bloat can mean a swollen feeling in your stomach. Look at the clues: is someone doing something, feeling something, or is a computer slow? The clues tell you which meaning to choose.
explain: Capital letters give clues too. In «Dory, Crush, Squirt, Bloat and Mr Ray are all based on real sea animals», the capital B shows that Bloat is a name, not a swelling. The sentence also says the film changes some things for the story, so the names are characters.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Bloat» (verb) means to swell up and become bigger than normal, usually with air, gas or liquid.
fact: «Bloat» (noun) means a swollen feeling in the stomach.
fact: Software bloat means too much extra stuff making a computer program big and slow.
fact: A capital letter in the middle of a sentence often shows a name, as in «Bloat» in «Dory, Crush, Squirt, Bloat and Mr Ray».
exercise: «Eating too fast can bloat your tummy.» What does bloat mean here? | To fall fast asleep | *To swell up and become bigger than normal | To laugh loudly | To run very fast
exercise: «The phone was slow because of software bloat.» What does bloat suggest? | *Too much extra stuff making it big and slow | A bright, glowing screen | A new and faster update | A tasty snack
exercise: In «Dory, Crush, Squirt, Bloat and Mr Ray are all based on real sea animals», what kind of word is Bloat? | A place where sea animals live | *A name of a character | A doing word meaning to swell | A colour
test: Which sentence uses bloat as a noun? | *Mia had a bloat after the big meal | The cows bloat when they eat too much grass | The balloon will bloat with air | Dad's laptop will bloat if we install too many apps
test: Why do we check the context when we meet bloat? | To count how many letters the word has | To decide whether it is a colour | *To find out which meaning fits the sentence | To see if it rhymes with another word
exam: What is the best way to work out the meaning of bloat in a new sentence? | Guess the first meaning you think of and never check | Count the letters in the word | *Look at the words around it and try each meaning in the sentence | Skip it and never read the sentence again
